; Schema-compile family, 3 of 4. `:step` is declared both as a common key of
; `track` and as a key of the `kick` variant. One name is one slot, so the
; two declarations would be indistinguishable at validation time — which
; type applies, and does the variant's requiredness override the common
; one? The schema is rejected instead of picking a rule.
;
; The emitter checks two collisions: variant-vs-common (this one) and
; variant-vs-earlier-variant. Only the first is exercised here — a document
; can carry the second only by declaring three variants, which would make
; the case about arithmetic rather than about the rule.
(plugin :name probe :version "1.0.0"
  (value-kind :name kind-tag
    :underlying symbol
    :members (member-set :values [kick bass]))
  (form :name track
    :discriminant kind
    (key :name kind :type kind-tag :optional true)
    (key :name step :type number :optional true)
    (variant :when kick
      (key :name step :type number :optional true))))

(track :kind kick)
